{"type": "thinking", "text": "With \"Show full date\" ON, group headings are unchanged (\"Due Thursday\" etc.) while ungrouped date chips show \"Nov 19\". Is this a bug? The heading \"Due Thursday\" is a relative weekday, which arguably is not the user's chosen date format. But maybe the headings are intended to be relative for the current week. Hmm.\n\nActually wait. Let's think about the changed feature more concretely: \"Due date grouping in the task list: group headings are now generated from the user's date format.\" This likely means headings used to be hardcoded e.g., \"Due %s\" with format; now they use a date formatter. A regression could produce wrong heading (e.g., \"Due 11/19/2026\" or missing \"Due\", or heading for wrong date). The visible headings look correct.\n\nBut maybe there's a bug when grouping by start date or created? The hint is specific to due date grouping.\n\nAnother thought: \"generated from the user's date format\" could refer to the date format in Settings > Date and time > \"Show full date\" (which is a boolean) or system locale date format. If the app is in a locale like English (US), headings \"November 24\" correct. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the regression is that headings are generated using a date format that includes weekday for all, even far dates? E.g., \"Due Tuesday, November 24\" includes weekday — is that \"the user's date format\"? The due date chip is \"Nov 24\" (no weekday) while heading \"Due Tuesday, November 24\" includes weekday. Maybe expected heading is \"Due Nov 24\"? Actually Tasks.org heading for due date groups might be \"Due Nov 24\" or \"Due Tuesday, November 24\"? I think Tasks.org shows headers like \"Due today\", \"Due tomorrow\", \"Due Nov 24\". Hmm.\n\nLet me consider the \"Show full date\" setting more: it's likely \"Show full date\" = show full date instead of relative (e.g., \"Thu\" → \"Nov 19\"). So group headings should follow: \"Due Thursday\" → \"Due Nov 19\" (or \"Due November 19\"). Since headings stayed \"Due Thursday\", this is inconsistent with the setting — and precisely matches \"group headings are now generated from the user's date format\" (user's date format = show full date preference). So likely the bug: due date group headings ignore the \"Show full date\" setting. Hmm, but is \"Show full date\" the \"user's date format\"? It's a user setting for date display. Quite plausible.\n\nLet me verify what \"Show full date\" does elsewhere: ungrouped chip changed. So the setting is the app's date format control. Group headings should presumably honor it per changed feature. But maybe the heading is supposed to be relative always? The setting turn-off revert: we should restore to off later.\n\nWait, there's another possibility: headings were intended to be generated from Android's date format pattern, and they are: \"Tuesday, November 24\" (full weekday + full month) vs due chip \"Nov 24\" (month abbrev). Maybe the heading uses \"EEEE, MMMM d\" while the user's chosen date format elsewhere uses \"MMM d\". The setting \"Show full date\" affects relative vs absolute, not pattern. Hmm.\n\nI need stronger evidence. Let me check how the heading would look with a different user-visible date format setting. Is there a date format setting in Settings > Look and feel? Maybe \"Date format\" options. Let me explore Look and feel. Also Task list options may have relevant settings (e.g., \"Show full date\"?).\n\nLet me check \"Look and feel\"."}
