{"type": "thinking", "text": "Last modified Ascending: Charge, Print, AAA → direction works for last modified. So subtask direction control is wired for \"By last modified\" but not for \"By title\" (and maybe Created). So the bug is specific to \"By title\" sorting ignoring direction, affecting both parent and subtask. That's the \"Name-based sorting: tasks are now sorted by a key produced by a shared helper\" regression! Great. One bug: title sort direction ignored. Both areas show it; I can report it once on the main list and mention subtasks, or report separately. Likely the expected single seeded bug is this. Let me confirm title direction on main list once more with a fresh toggle: currently Sorting By due date ascending. I can set Sorting By title Descending and confirm again (we already did twice, but let's keep evidence). Also test sorting by title Ascending vs Descending with the AAA subtask present? Parent AAA is subtask not top-level.\n\nNow let me explore other potential seeded bugs:\n\n1. Link handling: test different link forms. We can edit Read docs notes temporarily, adding e.g. \"www.example.com\" or \"example.com\" or \"(https://example.com/guide).\" and see if linkified. Also check task list snippet with links (not styled, probably intended).\n\nLet me test linkify behavior more: The changed feature says \"web links are now detected when the note is displayed.\" Maybe currently detection fails for text after clicking/editing, or only when the field loses focus. We saw it works. Potential bug: linkification is not applied after editing and saving without leaving screen? Or when Markdown is enabled? Hmm.\n\nTest unusual link values: link with trailing period \"See https://example.com/guide.\" Should the trailing period be excluded from link? Android Linkify handles that. Add to note and check. Also plain \"example.com\" (no scheme) — Linkify needs scheme by default unless match www. Tasks' show links might use Linkify.WEB_URLS which matches www. and schemes, not bare domains. That's expected.\n\nCould there be a bug where the link in note is detected but tapping opens nothing? We saw tapping focuses the note for editing. Maybe in the read-only display (when task is not in edit mode) there is no such screen. So not testable.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe the task edit screen notes field IS the display; clicking link while unfocused should open browser, and our tap focusing means the bug: link tap triggers edit mode instead of opening URL. In Tasks.org's actual behavior, I believe tapping the notes EditText when it's not focused enters edit mode (cursor), so links aren't directly tappable; maybe you tap and then link click works? Actually the Links setting \"Show links\" adds clickable links to notes when *viewing*. Let me research from memory: Tasks.org's EditTaskFragment notes field is a `EditText` with `setOnFocusChangeListener`? In old versions, \"Show links\" makes links clickable in the task edit screen. Tapping the note when not focused would focus and place cursor though. The link is displayed underlined; to open, maybe you tap and hold? Hmm.\n\nLet's test what tapping the note does vs tapping the link after it is not focused. We did: tap link → keyboard opens and note focused. So link not openable by tap. If the feature is \"web links are now detected when the note is displayed\", detection is visual; maybe the app also makes them tappable via custom movement method but the EditText focus intercepts. This could be the seeded bug: link detection styling works but clicking doesn't open. But without knowing intended behavior, risky to report.\n\nMaybe there is a separate \"task view\" screen: tapping the task title in the list might open a view (not edit)? We tapped row and got edit screen. There's no view screen.\n\nLet me check \"Task list options\" for \"Show notes\" or \"Task list\" display. Not needed.\n\n2. Due date grouping headings: possible bug. Let me look for anomalies with years or dates far away. The tasks are fixed. Heading \"Due Thursday\" versus chip \"Nov 19\"; that's by design (relative weekday). The changed hint might have seeded a bug: group headings generated from user's date format — maybe the heading for the current year date shows \"Due Tuesday, November 24\" but should be \"Due Tuesday, November 24\"?? Looks right. Maybe check grouping by due date in landscape or another screen (Today list). Hmm.\n\nCould the seeded bug be that headings are generated but the group order direction is wrong? We tested ascending/descending and they're right.\n\nOr maybe the heading uses a date format that includes the weekday in English, but with \"Show full date\" on, the heading still uses relative weekday. Compare: chips \"Nov 19\" (full) vs heading \"Due Thursday\" (relative) — inconsistency. If \"Show full date\" is the \"user's date format\" setting, this is a genuine bug: headings not generated from user's date format. But is \"Show full date\" really date format? Let me read its subtitle maybe off-screen? In Date and time, \"Show full date\" with no description. It likely means \"Show full date instead of relative dates (Today/Tomorrow)\". So yes, date format preference.\n\nLet me examine the heading when Show full date is OFF vs ON. OFF: Buy chip \"Thu\", heading \"Due Thursday\" (consistent). ON: chip \"Nov 19\", heading \"Due Thursday\" (inconsistent). Meanwhile Pack/Pay headings are full dates regardless. Hmm, but \"Due Thursday\" is a relative date, while \"Due Tuesday, November 24\" is absolute. For tasks within the next week, Tasks.org uses relative (today/tomorrow/weekday). With Show full date ON, the chip becomes \"Nov 19\" but heading remains \"Due Thursday\". That suggests headings ignore the setting. The hint \"group headings are now generated from the user's date format\" exactly points to this: heading should be generated using the date format (which show-full-date changes), but bug: it isn't.\n\nWait, but maybe \"user's date format\" refers to the Android system locale's date order (e.g., \"24 November\" vs \"November 24\"), and the app doesn't have a custom format setting besides Show full date. Hmm, there's \"Language\" setting. The heading \"November 24\" matches en-US format; chip \"Nov 24\" too. Hard to test other locales.\n\nI think a report about headings not respecting \"Show full date\" is plausible but might be rejected as intended behavior. Let me think about the exact phrase: \"Due date grouping in the task list: group headings are now generated from the user's date format.\" If the dev changed the heading generation to use a date format, the likely bug is a formatting issue visible in the headings, e.g., headings show \"Due Tuesday, November 24\" but the user's date format is \"MMM d\" (\"Nov 24\"), so the heading is wrong/too long; or the heading may show a *wrong date* due to timezone, e.g., \"Due Wednesday, November 25\" when due is Nov 24. Here heading matches actual due date (Nov 24 and chip Nov 24). So not wrong date.\n\nMaybe the heading should be \"Due Tue, Nov 24\"? We need a reference. In Tasks.org, I recall group headers in task list are like \"Due today\", \"Due tomorrow\", \"Due Nov 24\" — not full weekday. Hmm, actually Tasks.org date headers are strings from `DateStrings`: for due date group, it shows \"Due <dateString>\". `dateString` for within a week is like \"Thursday\"; beyond, \"Nov 24\" or \"November 24\"? Not sure.\n\nLet me search memory: Tasks.org old UI group headers: \"Due today\", \"Due tomorrow\", \"Due Friday\", \"Due Apr 5\". I think the app uses `DateStrings.getRelativeDateString` -> for date within a week returns weekday name, otherwise \"MMM d\". In the list due date chips, it shows \"Thu\", \"Nov 24\". The headings currently show \"Due Thursday\" (weekday) and \"Due Tuesday, November 24\" (weekday + full month). Wait, why would headings include weekday for far dates but chips don't? That's odd. A heading \"Due Tuesday, November 24\" is longer than the chip \"Nov 24\". Hmm, maybe that's the normal app behavior: group headers show a longer date including weekday, e.g., \"Due Tuesday, November 24\". I don't remember.\n\nActually maybe this IS the seeded bug: group headings for dates beyond a week show \"Due Tuesday, November 24\" instead of just \"Due November 24\"/\"Due Nov 24\". The changed area says headings generated from user's date format — ", "truncated": true}
