{"type": "thinking", "text": "Smart sort Ascending shows Charge, Print, AAA — reversed vs Descending (AAA, Charge, Print). So direction works for Smart sort. So the subtask direction is functional except title (same name sort bug). Good, so one root bug: title sorting ignores direction.\n\nThe \"Subtasks section controls built the same way for every option\" might be a red herring or the hint that all options have direction controls now (including My order). Hmm. Let's test My order: select My order, close, check order = manual (Charge, Print, AAA). Then toggle direction to Ascending/Descending and verify manual order stays. If direction control exists but is meaningless, is that a bug? Maybe report as UI issue: direction control for \"My order\" has no effect (by nature). But would a developer consider it a bug? The hint says \"its controls are now built the same way for every option\" — this suggests the seeded bug could be precisely that the \"My order\" option now displays an active direction toggle, which does nothing. Let's test and possibly report. But \"no effect\" is expected for manual order; still, showing a control that does nothing is a usability bug. Yet the task expects QA sweep of \"sorting and grouping options\" — so flagging meaningless control may be valid.\n\nWait, maybe there's a subtler bug: For \"My order\", the direction toggle isn't just meaningless — changing it might alter the *sorting* of tasks in some other list, or the label might be wrong (\"Descending\" default). Hmm.\n\nLet me look at the main Sorting sheet: \"My order\" is disabled for My Tasks but might be available in a user list. Not relevant.\n\nAnother thought: The subtasks section control could be mislabeled: The row might be titled \"Subtasks\" but represents \"sort subtasks\" — fine. For \"My order\", in Tasks.org, the direction toggle is likely hidden. I'll test My order UI and decide."}
