{"type": "thinking", "text": "Default list view shows only Link test and AAA test. So the four precondition tasks are indeed not members of Default list; they are \"unlisted\" tasks that show in My Tasks. The list chip on Buy milk shows \"Default list\" but that's likely the fallback display of the default selection in the picker, not actual membership.\n\nSo grouping by List shows only list members; unlisted tasks are omitted. Is that a bug? It's arguably expected for a list-grouping view to only include tasks in lists. But the tasks are *in* My Tasks, so showing none under any header is odd. In Tasks.org, I'd expect tasks without a list to be under \"No list\". Hmm. But the developer couldn't reproduce; likely they consider it fine/not a bug. I'll drop this.\n\nHowever, this insight matters for sorting: the four precondition tasks are unlisted, but they display under My Tasks. Fine.\n\nNow, since the report was not confirmed, I shouldn't refile. Let me focus on the seeded bugs.\n\nWhat else? Let's revisit the changed areas:\n1. **Link handling in task notes**: we verified detection and clicking \"Open\" works. Need to check \"Edit\" option behavior (edit link). Also check link display after reopening task/saving; rotation; and multiple/precondition link opens correctly (example.com/guide). Also test with \"Show links\" OFF (default) — since setting was off by default, is that a bug? The feature \"web links are now detected when the note is displayed\" is gated by a setting that is off by default. Hmm, maybe the seed bug is that the setting doesn't persist? Let's test persistence: we turned Show links ON earlier; it should persist. Open Read docs and see underlined link now (without touching setting). Yes, we already saw Link test links styled; Show links still on. Persistence works.\n\nBut wait — did we verify that the detection uses the \"Show links\" setting name \"Add links to websites, addresses, and phone numbers\"? That setting is likely for phone/address too. Not our concern.\n\n2. **Name-based sorting**: confirmed direction bug. Also test case sensitivity/collation? Let's test with tasks of different cases and accented names to see if the shared helper produces a sensible key. Since we have Link test and AAA test, we can create additional temp tasks? We already have temp data to delete. Could rename AAA test to lowercase or test with names like \"apple\", \"Banana\". The helper \"key produced by a shared helper\" might lowercase the name; potential bug could be that it strips spaces/punctuation or uses a weird key causing wrong order. The direction bug is likely the seeded one though.\n\nLet me test sort by title ascending with mixed case to see if uppercase/lowercase sorts naturally (case-insensitive). Create tasks \"apple\", \"Banana\", \"cherry\" quickly? That's three tasks; tedious but doable. Alternatively rename existing test tasks: Link test → \"apple\", AAA test → \"Banana\". Then title ascending order should be apple, Banana, Buy milk, ... hmm. We can rename AAA test and Link test, but we need to delete them later anyway. But changing names of temp tasks is fine. However our direction bug evidence used current names; already filed. Let me test mixed-case sorting using temp tasks.\n\nActually, the shared helper key might lowercase (for case-insensitive sorting) or normalize accents. A seeded bug could be that it sorts uppercase before lowercase (ASCII order), so \"AAA test\" sorts before \"apple\" vs case-insensitive \"apple\" before \"Banana\". We can test with existing: \"AAA test\" starts uppercase A, \"Link test\" L, others B, P, R. Case-insensitive ordering: AAA test, Buy milk, Link test, Pack, Pay, Read. ASCII: AAA test, Buy milk, Link test, ... same. Not distinguishing.\n\nLet's create two temp tasks \"apple\" and \"Banana\" via FAB. Actually renaming existing test tasks is quicker: open AAA test, rename to \"aardvark\"; open Link test, rename to \"Zebra\". Then title ascending expected: aardvark, Buy milk, Pack, Pay, Read, Zebra (case-insensitive). If ASCII/case-sensitive with uppercase first: Buy, Pack, Pay, Read, Zebra, aardvark. Hmm, lowercase 'a' (97) > uppercase 'Z' (90), so case-sensitive ascending puts Zebra before aardvark. That would reveal a case-sensitive key bug. But is that a bug? Users expect case-insensitive name sort. Tasks.org likely uses Collator (case-insensitive). The changelog \"key produced by a shared helper\" suggests maybe helper builds key with lowercase and locale; a seeded bug could break case handling.\n\nHmm, but the known direction bug is a strong candidate for the seeded name-sort bug. Testing case might reveal another bug, but could also be intended. Let's test quickly since it's cheap: rename temp tasks.\n\nFirst navigate back to My Tasks (from Default list). Then open AAA test, change title to \"aardvark\", save. Then Link test → \"Zebra\", save. Then sort By title Ascending and observe. But note current grouping List; switch None and sorting title ascending.\n\nActually, maybe rename to more informative: \"banana\" (lowercase b) and \"Apple\" (uppercase A). Case-insensitive ascending: Apple, banana, Buy milk... Case-sensitive: Apple (65) < Buy (66) < banana (98). Hmm, that order is same as case-insensitive (Apple, banana, Buy would be A,b,B → Apple, banana, Buy if case-insensitive). To distinguish, use \"Zebra\" (uppercase Z, 90) and \"aardvark\" (lowercase a, 97). Case-insensitive: aardvark, Buy, ..., Zebra. Case-sensitive ASCII: Buy..., Zebra, aardvark. Good.\n\nBut wait, current task \"AAA test\" has priority Low and is a temp task. Rename AAA test → \"Zebra\". Link test → \"aardvark\"? Renaming Link test is fine (temp). Then ascending expected aardvark first, Zebra last (case-insensitive), or Zebra before aardvark (case-sensitive).\n\nLet's do. Navigate to My Tasks first."}
