{"type": "thinking", "text": "Ascending title order: Buy milk, Pack for trip, Pay rent, Read docs, aardvark, Zebra. That's NOT case-insensitive: case-insensitive would be aardvark first, then Buy, Pack, Pay, Read, Zebra. The actual order places \"aardvark\" after \"Read docs\" (i.e., lowercase 'a' sorts after uppercase letters) and \"Zebra\" last (uppercase Z after lowercase a? wait ASCII: 'Z'=90, 'a'=97, so case-sensitive ASCII would put Zebra BEFORE aardvark). Actual: aardvark then Zebra. That's not ASCII either!\n\nHmm: sequence: Buy (B), Pack (P), Pay (P), Read (R), aardvark (a), Zebra (Z). In case-insensitive collation, aardvark (a) should come first. In ASCII case-sensitive: B (66), P (80), R (82), Z (90), a (97) → Zebra then aardvark. Actual: aardvark then Zebra → neither.\n\nInteresting! It seems the sort key might be something like lowercase then... let's see: Maybe the key is built by taking the first letter's lowercase? a→a, Z→z: a then z at end. But why are uppercase-starting tasks B/P/R before a? If case-insensitive, a should be before b. So it's like: keys are aggregated: B, P, R, a, Z? Wait actual order: Buy, Pack, Pay, Read, aardvark, Zebra. If keys were case-sensitive but with uppercase and lowercase groups: uppercase letters sorted A-Z first, then lowercase a-z. That's ASCII: B, P, R, Z, a — but Zebra appears after aardvark, so not ASCII.\n\nUnless \"Zebra\" key is somehow \"zebra\" lowercased while \"aardvark\" key is \"aardvark\", and the sort is locale Collator with uppercase before lowercase? ICU Collator default (tertiary) would compare case-insensitively first then case as tiebreak: aardvark (a) vs Buy (b): a < b, so aardvark first. Not matching.\n\nMaybe the helper key is the *first character* of the title? B, P, P, R, a, Z. A locale collator usually case-insensitive primary: a < b < p < r < z → aardvark first. Not matching.\n\nHmm, actual order has aardvark after Read but before Zebra. What key order could produce that? If key = lowercase first char: b, p, p, r, a, z → a first. No.\n\nIf key = uppercase? \"AARDVARK\"? then a first.\n\nWhat if the helper produces a key by stripping the first letter? No.\n\nWait, maybe the list isn't sorted by title at all for the last two because they're \"new\" tasks and sort is stale? Or because \"Move completed tasks to bottom\"? No. Could it be that the *sort key* for new tasks is empty/null and those sort at the end, with secondary order by creation: aardvark before Zebra? Hmm! That's plausible: The shared helper produces a sort key stored when task is saved; tasks created before the helper change (the 4 preconditions) have keys computed, and new tasks... hmm.\n\nActually the observed order Buy, Pack, Pay, Read, aardvark, Zebra is suspicious: the 4 preconditions first (alphabetical), then the two new tasks (creation order). Maybe the helper key for new tasks is null, so they sink to the bottom.\n\nLet's test with a new task starting with \"A\" — if it still goes to the bottom, then new tasks' keys are null/unset, which is a serious sorting bug (new tasks not sorted by name). Or maybe our two new tasks have sort keys computed incorrectly due to being created with the old sort setting? Hmm.\n\nLet's test: create a new task \"Aardvark2\" (uppercase A) and see if it sorts first or last. If last, the key column is null for newly created tasks. But also note the precondition \"Play\" order shows Buy, Pack, Pay (correct alphabetically: Pack < Pay because c<y). Fine.\n\nAnother angle: maybe \"aaa\" vs \"Zebra\" locale sorting in ICU with \"en-US\" sorts lowercase after uppercase in a \"case-first\" collation? Wait, ICU default collation for en-US: \"a\" < \"A\" < \"b\" < \"B\"? In ICU root collation, lower-case sorts before upper-case as a tertiary difference, but primary letters compare case-insensitively: aardvark vs Buy: primary 'a' vs 'b' → aardvark first. So no.\n\nUnless the helper lowercases with a weird transformation that maps 'a' to '~'? Hmm.\n\nMaybe the helper uses a \"normalized key\" like first character repeated? Let's consider it uses `String.compareTo` on a key that includes the original name but with a prefix indicating something (e.g., priority?). The \"shared helper\" could generate keys like \"buymilk\", \"packfortrip\"... then compareTo (case-sensitive ASCII): \"buymilk\" < \"packfortrip\" < \"payrent\" < \"readdocs\" < \"aardvark\"? No: 'b' < 'p' < 'r' < 'a'? No, 'a' is smallest, so aardvark first.\n\nWhat if the key is the *reverse* string? \"drowtraa\" vs \"yub\" etc. Hmm: reverse of aardvark = \"kravdraa\"; reverse of Zebra = \"arbeZ\"; Buy milk = \"klim yub\"; Pack = \"kcap\"; Pay rent=\"tner yap\"; Read docs=\"scod daer\". Sorted ASCII: \"arbeZ\" (Zebra reverse), \"kcap\", \"klim yub\", \"kravdraa\", \"scod daer\", \"tner yap\" — not matching.\n\nMaybe the sort key is computed by stripping leading spaces/lowercasing and the comparator is applied only to the first character in a locale-sensitive way with \"case level off\"? Not matching.\n\nCould the list actually be ordered by *due date* (sorting setting didn't apply) with a tiebreak? Due dates: Buy Nov19, Pack Nov24, Pay Dec4, Read none, aardvark none, Zebra Nov13. If sorted by due date ascending, Zebra (Nov13) would first. Not matching.\n\nCould it be ordered by *created* (old tasks first, new tasks later) then title? The 4 old tasks: Buy, Pack, Pay, Read alphabetical; then new tasks aardvark, Zebra (created order). Hmm, coincidence? The 4 old tasks' creation dates were null/1970 equal; then secondary title. New tasks created later. So sort By title with null/empty sort keys for old tasks? Eh.\n\nWait, maybe the precondition tasks have *sort keys* already (from data), and new tasks get keys only after some event. When we changed AAA test title to aardvark and saved, the key may not have been regenerated (so it retained the key for \"AAA test\" = \"aaa test\"). Link test → Zebra retained key \"link test\". Then sorted by title using the *old cached keys*: aardvark's key \"aaa test\" would sort FIRST, but it appears after Read docs. Hmm no.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the key includes the task's UUID? weird.\n\nLet me not over-theorize. Let me test more concretely: create a brand-new task \"Aaa new\" (uppercase A) and another \"zzz new\" and see where they sort. But creating two more tasks is more cleanup. We already have two temp tasks; we can rename them to controlled names again. Renaming may not update the key though (which itself could be a bug!). Interesting: if renaming a task does not update its sort key, then sorting by name uses a stale key. But the app likely recomputes on save.\n\nActually! Big realization: The order Buy, Pack, Pay, Read, aardvark, Zebra *could* be explained if the sort key is the original *title at creation* (or the last \"sort key\" sync) for the two new tasks: \"AAA test\" and \"Link test\" keys. Sorted ascending case-insensitive: AAA test, Buy milk, Link test, Pack, Pay, Read docs. That would be AAA, Buy, Link, Pack, Pay, Read — not matching (aardvark/AAA appears after Read). If case-sensitive ASCII: AAA (65...) first. No.\n\nWhat if key is lowercase original: \"aaa test\", \"buy milk\", \"link test\", \"pack...\", \"pay...\", \"read...\" → aaa first. No.\n\nWhat if keys for the two new tasks are empty (null) and the comparator places empty keys last? That fits! Both new tasks have null/empty sort keys → sorted last, and among them stable order by... aardvark before Zebra? The order of creation: aardvark (AAA test) created before Zebra (Link test) → matches. And the four preconditions have computed keys sorted A-Z → Buy, Pack, Pay, Read. This fits perfectly.\n\nSo the bug: newly created tasks have no name sort key, so By title sorting places them at the end regardless of name. Since we created the new tasks *before* switching sort to By title... hmm, the key should be computed at creation. But maybe the key is only generated when the task is *displayed/sorted* by an older code path, and this build's shared helper fails to store keys for new tasks? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the key is the lowercase collation key computed and stored in the DB at task creation. For our new tasks, the key may correspond to the *title at creation*: \"AAA test\" and \"Link test\". Sorted keys: aaa test, buy milk, link test, pack for trip, pay rent, read docs → AAA test sho", "truncated": true}
