{"type": "thinking", "text": "\"Apple pie\" went to the very bottom, after Zebra, even though By title Ascending should place it first (or among A). This confirms: newly created tasks are NOT sorted by title at all; they're appended at the end. That is a serious bug in the changed \"name-based sorting\" feature — likely the actual seeded bug (or at least a different aspect of it).\n\nWait, but the new task was created while sorting By title Ascending. It appended at the bottom. Then if I leave and come back or re-select sort, does it get keyed? Let's test: re-open sort sheet, re-select By title (or switch to another sort and back), then see whether Apple pie moves. If it stays at bottom, the key is null and sorting is broken for new tasks. Very reportable: new tasks sort last regardless of name.\n\nAlso note ascending order of existing: Buy, Pack, Pay, Read, aardvark, Zebra, Apple pie. Hmm, \"aardvark\" and \"Zebra\" are both user-created through the same FAB (created as subtask and task) — wait aardvark was originally created *as a subtask* of Pack (via Add subtask), while Zebra (Link test) was created via FAB. Both at bottom in creation order. Apple pie (FAB) also bottom. So all newly created tasks appended. The precondition tasks are sorted alphabetically. \n\nCould the app be sorting by *created date* primarily and ignoring title? The order Buy, Pack, Pay, Read, aardvark, Zebra, Apple pie: the first four are preconditions in alphabetical order, which also might be their creation order? Unknown. If sort is by creation ascending, preconditions created first (in some order), then new ones. The precondition creation order might not be alphabetical though (Buy milk, Pack for trip, Pay rent, Read docs — could be creation order as listed in preconditions! The list order given is Buy milk, Pack for trip, Pay rent, Read docs — exactly the observed order). Hmm!! The preconditions list is stated as \"Buy milk (due in 5 days), Pack for trip (due in 10 days), Pay rent (due in 20 days), Read docs (no due date)\" — that's likely their creation order. So the current \"By title ascending\" order could actually be **Created ascending**! Let me check the sort sheet state: we set Sorting By title Ascending. But maybe the list is still sorted by the previous sort (By due date? no). Hmm.\n\nWait, the FAB-created Apple pie went to the bottom, consistent with Created ascending. And aardvark (created as subtask earlier) then Zebra (created via FAB) then Apple pie — creation order: AAA test (aardvark) created first, Link test (Zebra) second, Apple pie third. Yes! So the list is sorted by Created ascending, not By title. But the sort sheet says By title Ascending. Did switching to By title fail to apply? Earlier, when we set Grouping None + Sorting By title (first time), the order was Buy milk, Pack for trip, Pay rent, Read docs — which coincides with creation order! So we never actually verified title sorting works at all with distinct creation-order data. The apparent alphabetical order was also creation order. Then with title descending, the order stayed created ascending. That is consistent with **title sorting not being applied at all; the list remains in Created order**. Hmm, but the direction bug we filed said title descending ignored; maybe actually the entire title sort is ignored, and the order we see is something else (created/manual). The dev confirmed the direction bug, so maybe partial.\n\nNow with Apple pie, By title Ascending should put Apple pie first but it's last → title sort is completely ignored for new tasks? Or for all? The preconditions' creation order happens to be alphabetical, so we couldn't tell before. The list is in Created ascending. So maybe the \"By title\" sort is entirely not applied, and what we see is the default/my order? Hmm.\n\nBut wait: earlier we set Sorting By title Descending and the top-level list showed Buy, Pack, Pay, Read, AAA, Link — creation order. Yes.\n\nThen we set Sorting By due date Descending and saw Read docs, Pay, Pack, Buy — due date order worked.\n\nSo title sort appears to be ignored entirely (not just direction). Why did the developer confirm the direction issue? The dev confirmed \"thanks, confirmed and logged\" — maybe they logged the underlying bug.\n\nLet's verify: with Sorting By title Ascending and Apple pie existing, if title sort worked, order would be Apple pie, aardvark, Buy milk, Pack for trip, Pay rent, Read docs, Zebra (case-insensitive) or some variant. Observed: Buy, Pack, Pay, Read, aardvark, Zebra, Apple pie = creation order. So title sort is definitely not working (or at least new tasks aren't included). \n\nHmm, but maybe the sort key is computed and stored, and the SQL query sorts by `sortKey` but tasks created before/after have null sortKey. Precondition tasks might have sortKey null too, and the SQL `ORDER BY sortKey` with nulls? If all null, order would be arbitrary (rowid = creation order). That fits: none of the tasks have sort keys; order is rowid. But then the \"By title\" sort never worked. Yet the dev said confirmed... Maybe the dev confirmed because the direction indeed doesn't work, but the root is missing keys.\n\nEither way, we should file a *new* report with this stronger evidence: \"By title sorting does not sort by title at all; newly created task Apple pie stays last.\" But this is essentially the same area; the dev may have already logged. However, a separate report with different symptom (missing/incorrect sort) could be valuable. The original report was about direction; this is about new tasks not sorted at all. But they might be the same root; dev said confirmed and logged. Since we shouldn't spam, but the new evidence shows the bug is more severe and includes new tasks. Hmm.\n\nActually, wait. Let me carefully verify that title sorting is being applied to the precondition tasks by creating a task that is definitely out of creation order but with an alphabetically-late name, then see. Apple pie (early A) went last → title sort ignored. Also, the existing order matches creation order. This is conclusive: By title sorting is not ordering by title for these tasks.\n\nBut hold on — maybe the list is currently sorted by \"My order\" because the \"By title\" selection isn't saved? Let me re-open the sort sheet and verify Sorting = By title Ascending."}
