{"type": "thinking", "text": "Dev: already logged. Good, so the name sort bug is known (our first report likely covered it or the seeded bug). Now let's continue testing other areas.\n\nRemaining areas:\n- Link handling: we tested detection and Open. Also test \"Edit\" option in the link dialog, and the precondition link. Also test default setting OFF maybe. And test persistence after rotate/reopen. There might be a seeded bug not yet found: e.g., links in notes don't survive editing? Let's test more.\n- Subtasks sort sheet controls: maybe a UI bug remains. We tested direction for title bug (already logged). Need to check \"controls built the same way for every option\" maybe something like the direction toggle for \"My order\" being meaningless. Could be the seeded bug that hasn't been confirmed yet. Let me explore further.\n\nLet me think about likely seeded bug in subtasks section: \"its controls are now built the same way for every option.\" What could go wrong? Maybe for one option (e.g., \"My order\"), the direction control is now shown and *toggling it re-sorts the parent tasks* or *crashes*. Or maybe the row's title label is wrong for some options. We saw all options show correct names and direction controls.\n\nWait, maybe there's a specific bug: when Subtasks option is \"My order\", the direction control displays a direction (\"Descending\") and can be toggled, but manual order should not be reversible. That's \"controls built the same way for every option\" — previously My order had no direction control; now it does, which is wrong. This is exactly the kind of regression that phrase hints at. The dev might confirm it. Let me examine the main Sorting section: \"My order\" (disabled) has no separate direction control. In the subtasks section, \"My order\" is a valid option and shows a direction control. Hmm, but for manual order, direction is meaningless. Is showing it definitely a bug? It's a UI inconsistency. The phrase \"its controls are now built the same way for every option\" suggests the intent was to make controls consistent, but the regression could be that they're *too* uniform — including direction for My order.\n\nLet me search memory of Tasks.org: In the sort dialog, each section (Grouping, Sorting, Subtasks, Completed) has a row with title, current value, and an ascending/descending toggle. I believe the toggle is always shown, even for \"My order\"/manual sort? In old Tasks.org, the \"My order\" option is only available for user lists and the direction toggle is hidden? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively the bug could be that for \"My order\" the direction toggle is *enabled* and tapping it causes the subtask order to reverse (manual order reversed) — that would definitely be a bug. We tested: My order Descending showed Charge, Print, aardvark (manual order) — not reversed. So direction has no effect, just meaningless.\n\nHmm, maybe with the current temp AAA→aardvark subtask detached, Pack has only 2 subtasks Charge, Print. My order vs direction not observable but manual order was Charge, Print either way.\n\nLet me look for another subtasks bug: maybe the Subtasks sorting only applies when grouping is None? We tested both None and grouped (the Pack subtasks order within group). With sort By title, subtasks were AAA, Charge, Print in both cases. Direction ignored (same root).\n\nWait, we tested subtask *direction* with By title and observed no effect. Then with By last modified it worked. So the bug is only title (already logged). The \"controls\" hint might just be context for the same title bug (since subtask sort uses the same control). Hmm, the changelog lists four changes; the seeded bugs may be 2-4 across them. We found name sort bug (logged). Due date grouping appears fine. Link handling appears fine. Maybe there's still one in link handling or subtasks.\n\nLet's dig into link handling more. Potential seeded bug: links are detected only when \"Show links\" setting is ON, but the setting default OFF means